Thread --force-disable-user-env through to ptyHost's own shell-env resolution

getResolvedShellEnv() (shellEnv.ts) checks args['force-disable-user-env']
to skip spawning a real shell entirely -- but PtyHostService's own
_resolveShellEnv() was passing a hardcoded `{ _: [] }` instead of the
real parsed argv, so the flag could never reach it regardless of what was
passed on the command line. NodeAgentHostStarter's own _resolveShellEnv
already does this correctly (passes this._environmentService.args).

Confirmed as a real, reproducing gap via a captured node-stdio.log
("ptyHost was unable to resolve shell environment ... spawn sh ENOENT")
even with --force-disable-user-env already passed on the command line --
not guessed. iOS denies spawning `sh` at all (same sandbox wall as
fork()), so this resolveShellEnv attempt was always doomed here
regardless of the flag; on a real Unix host it's merely a wasted shell
spawn on every ptyHost start, since --force-disable-user-env exists
specifically to skip it.

No DI changes: PtyHostService has no environment service injected, and
adding one purely for this one flag would widen the blast radius of
every other PtyHostService construction site. process.argv is the same
source of truth NativeParsedArgs itself is built from.

